==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|mylky}}, {{m|enm|melky}}, equivalent to {{suf|en|milk|y|id2=adjectival}}. Cognate with {{cog|de|milchig|t=milky}}, {{cog|sv|mjölkig|t=milky}}. {{doublet|en|milchig}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GenAm|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈmɪlki/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-milky.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪlki|s=2}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|er}}

# Resembling [[milk]] in [[color]], [[consistency]], [[smell]], etc.; consisting of milk.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Hamlet|II|ii|text=his sword,<br>Which was declining on the '''milky''' head<br>Of reverend Priam, seem'd i' the air to stick:}}
#* {{RQ:Pope Iliad|16|line=780|page=267|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004836009.0001.004|text=The Pails high-foaming with a '''milky''' Flood,}}
#* '''1731''', {{w|John Arbuthnot}}, ''An Essay concerning the Nature of Aliments'', London: J. Tonson, Chapter 3, Prop. 3, p. 51,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004889407.0001.000]</sup>
#*: {{...}} some Plants upon breaking their Vessels yield a '''milky''' Juice; others a Yellow of peculiar Tastes and Qualities.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1928|author=w:Radclyffe Hall|title=w:The Well of Loneliness|section=Book One, Chapter Three, 3|url=http://gutenberg.net.au/ebooks06/0609021h.html
|passage={{...}} the kind, slightly '''milky''' odour of cattle {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1980|author=w:J. M. Coetzee|title=w:Waiting for the Barbarians|chapter=2|pages=37–38|publisher=Penguin|year_published=1999
|passage=She wheels her gaze from the wall on to me. The black irises are set off by '''milky''' whites as clear as a child’s.}}
# {{lb|en|color science|informal}} Of the [[black]] in an [[image]], appearing as dark [[gray]] rather than black.
# {{lb|en|of a drink}} Containing (an especially large amount of) milk.
#: '''''milky''' tea; '''milky''' cocoa''
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1959|author=w:Muriel Spark|title={{w|Memento Mori (novel)|Memento Mori}}|location=New York|publisher=New Directions|year_published=2000|chapter=1|page=13|url=https://archive.org/details/mementomori00spar_0
|passage=Mrs. Anthony, their daily housekeeper, brought in the '''milky''' coffee and placed it on the breakfast table.}}
#* {{RQ:Burgess Clockwork Orange|chapter=5|year=1963|page=156|passage={{...}} we sat down {{...}} to the old crack crack crack of eggs and the crackle crunch crunch of this black toast, very '''milky''' chai standing by in [[bolshy]] great morning mugs.}}
# {{lb|en|of grains}} Containing a [[whitish]] [[liquid]], [[juicy]].
#* '''1800''', {{w|Robert Bloomfield}}, ''The Farmer’s Boy'', London: Vernor & Hood ''et al.'', “Summer,” p. 30,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004812350.0001.000]</sup>
#*: Shot up from broad rank blades that droop below,
#*: The nodding <small>WHEAT-EAR</small> forms a graceful bow,
#*: With '''milky''' kernels starting full, weigh’d down,
#*: Ere yet the sun hath ting’d its head with brown;
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1914|author=w:Robert W. Chambers|title=The Hidden Children|location=New York|publisher=Appleton|chapter=19|page=575|url=https://archive.org/details/hiddenchildren00chamrich
|passage={{...}} the servile Eries were staggering out of the corn fields laden with ripe ears; and the famished soldiers were shouting and cursing at them and tearing the corn from their arms to gnaw the raw and '''milky''' grains.}}
#* '''1981''', Martin Morolong, “The Old-Style Calendar” in {{w|Bessie Head}}, ''Serowe: Village of the Rain Wind'', London: Heinemann,
#*: The birds perch on the sorghum heads and try to eat them but the dry seed falls to the ground. The birds can only peck it out of the sorghum head when it is still '''milky''' and green.
# {{lb|en|colloquial}} [[cowardly|Cowardly]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Timon of Athens|III|1|text=Has friendship such a faint and '''milky''' heart?}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1938|author=w:Graham Greene|title={{w|Brighton Rock (novel)|Brighton Rock}}|publisher=Vintage|year_published=2002|section=Part , Chapter , pp. 45-46|url=https://archive.org/details/brightonrockvint00gree
|passage=‘Who said there was going to be any killing?’ The lightning flared up and showed his tight shabby jacket, the bunch of soft hair at the nape. ‘I’ve got a date, that’s all. You be careful what you say, Spicer. You aren’t '''milky''', are you?’<br>‘I’m not '''milky'''. You got me wrong, Pinkie. I just don’t want another killing {{...}}’}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=23 Apr 77|journal=Gay Community News|page=5|author=Ken Withers|title=No Boycott|text=The boycott of Florida orange juice, while coming from genuine feelings, is basically a '''milky'''-liberal response to an issue that needs united, vocal, public action.}}
# {{lb|en|colloquial}} [[immature|Immature]], [[childish]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1651|author=w:William Davenant|title=w:Gondibert|location=London|publisher=John Holden|section=Book 2, Canto 3, Stanza 48, p. 101|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A37179.0001.001
|passage=Gone is your fighting Youth, whom you have bred<br>From '''milkie''' Childhood to the years of bloud!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1851|author=w:Charles Kingsley|title={{w|Yeast (novel)|Yeast}}|location=London|publisher=John W. Parker|chapter=1|page=15|url=https://archive.org/details/yeastaproblem00kinggoog
|passage=There were the everlasting hills around, even as they had grown for countless ages, beneath the still depths of the primeval chalk ocean, in the '''milky''' youth of this great English land.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1882|author=w:Walter Besant|title=The Revolt of Man|location=London|publisher=Blackwood|chapter=2|page=45|url=https://archive.org/details/revoltman00besagoog
|passage=“I am no '''milky''', modest, obedient youth, Constance. {{...}}”}}
#* {{RQ:Lewis Babbitt|chapter=29|section=III|page=337|url=https://archive.org/details/babbittle00lewi|passage=He got so thoroughly into the jocund spirit that he didn’t much mind seeing Tanis drooping against the shoulder of the youngest and '''milkiest''' of the young men {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Producing milk, [[lactate|lactating]].
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|I|viii|passage=As great a noyse, as when in Cymbrian plaine<br>An heard of Bulles, whom kindly rage doth sting,<br>Doe for the '''milky''' mothers want complaine,<br>And fill the fieldes with troublous bellowing,}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1648|author={{w|Robert Herrick (poet)|Robert Herrick}}|chapter=A Country Life|title=w:Hesperides|location=London|publisher=John Williams and Francis Eglesfield|page=37|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A43441.0001.001
|passage={{...}} ye heare the Lamb by many a bleat<br>Woo’d to come suck the '''milkie''' Teat:}}

